Kubernetes service: cluster IP

a full stack web application typically has different kinds of pods hosting fifferent parts of application 

a no of pods running front end
a no of pods running back end
a no of pods running database

the web front end pods needs to communicate backend pods and backend pods needs to communicate to database pods

what is the right way to eastablish connectivity b/w these tiers

the pods all of them will have ip address assigned to them
these ips are static till a pod is down or deleted and new pod will be created all the time

so you cannot rely on these ips to communicate internally

now the pod2 in frontend web tier pods needs to communicate to a backend pod in backend tier pods
which one among the pods will it conect to and who decides that 

a service, can help us groups the pod together and provide a single interface to access the pod in a group

a service created to group all the backend pod together and provide a single interface for tohter pods to access this service
the request is forwarded to one of the pod randomly

similarly created one more service and allow the backend pod to access redis service

this enables to easily deploy a microservice application in a kubernetes cluster

each layer(group of pods) can easily scale up or down, without requiring manual intervention using replicaset and communication b/w various services(group of pods) 

each service gets a ip and name assigned inside the cluster and that is the name that should be used by other paths to access the service 

this type of service is known as cluster ip

to create such service use definition

apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata: 
  name: backend
spec:
  type: ClusterIP 
  #by default if you dont specify type, it will take clusterIP
  ports:
    - targetport: 80
    #targetport is where backend pods are listening on
      port: 80
    #port is where the clusterip service is exposed and listening
  selector: 
  #to link the service to set of pods we use selector
    app: redis
    type: database

#create the service using
kubectl create -f service-definition.yaml

#to view the service
kubectl get services

the service can be access by other pods using the cluster ip or servicename
bacause the cluster IP/servicename is permanent until manually deleted


